#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1456cdfb-6ae9-5439-8ebe-e5b41687dfa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5337" :question/problem "What is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/54dd85489d2ff3c834788cc817285762.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(14,2)$ and passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(12,6\\right)\n$$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (14,2)\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4p(x-14)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p$. Since the parabola passes through the point $(12,6)$, we substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(12 - 14) \\\\\n16 &= 4p(-2) \\\\\np &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0$. So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\n(0 - 2)^{2} &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\n4 &= -8(x - 14) \\\\\nx - 14 &= -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2} + 14 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{27}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{27}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "77303537-c614-5269-a22e-0bd412e7fa99" :question/math-academy-id "e-5339" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola $(y-2)^2=4(x+1)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the points of intersection of the parabola with the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(x + 1) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(0 + 1) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny - 2 &= ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 2 ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis $(0,0)$ and $(0,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "4ce7b936-de41-5aeb-b980-80d9fa91314f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5340" :question/problem "What are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/a0f5f271f1e4f257f3deea9c3842dcf2.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(7,-4)$ and passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(5,-6\\right)\n$$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2=4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nand substituting\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (7,-4)\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+4)^2=4p(x-7)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p$. Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,-6)$, we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-6 + 4)^{2} &= 4p(5 - 7) \\\\\n4 &= 4p(-2) \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n(y + 4)^{2} =-2(x - 7)\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x= 0$. So, we substitute $x= 0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 4)^{2} &= -2(0 - 7) \\\\\n(y + 4)^{2} &= 14 \\\\\ny + 4 &= ± \\sqrt{14} \\\\\ny &= -4 ± \\sqrt{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-4-\\sqrt{14}$ and $-4+\\sqrt{14}$."}]}
